WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:05.820
To finish this section on data protection,&nbsp;
let's look at some predefined security roles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:11.340
within SQL Server. Roles have a big purpose, and&nbsp;
that is, to have pre-defined sets of permissions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:16.860
that are applied to users, so that, in theory,&nbsp;
individual permissions do not need to be used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:23.640
and thus tracked within both a server instance and&nbsp;
its databases, streamlining security with users.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:30.420
As I expand the Security folder and then Server&nbsp;
Roles in this instance of SQL Server, understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:36.480
that for the exam the main focus in on the purpose&nbsp;
of roles, not having to know each individual role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:42.420
but having an idea of each role will help define&nbsp;
the overall purpose of roles. There are nine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:49.020
pre-defined server roles and I am going to cover&nbsp;
these in the order of least restrictive to most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:55.500
restrictive. First, we have sysadmin. One with&nbsp;
this role can perform any activity on the server,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.500 --> 00:01:01.020
so this role should be used sparingly. Next,&nbsp;
we have the serveradmin role, which allows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:06.600
one to change server configurations and shut&nbsp;
down the server. The third role to look at is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:12.900
securityadmin. A user in this role can grant and&nbsp;
revoke permissions on a server level and do the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:19.860
same for databases on which they have permissions.&nbsp;
The fourth role to look at is processadmin, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:25.860
allows one to end running processes on a server,&nbsp;
such as a backup process that is taking far longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:32.340
than anticipated. Next we have the setupadmin&nbsp;
role, which allows one to add and remove linked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:37.020
servers, which allow one to use queries to&nbsp;
get information from remote data sources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:44.520
such as another SQL server instance. The bulkadmin&nbsp;
role allows one to use a bulk insert statement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:52.020
which imports large amounts of data into a&nbsp;
database. The diskadmin role allows one to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:59.580
manage disk files. The dbcreator role allows one&nbsp;
to create, alter, drop, and restore a database.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:05.580
If you are wondering about a backup role,&nbsp;
you will see where it is and why soon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:11.640
Lastly, we have the public role, which is a&nbsp;
role that is assigned to everyone on a server,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:17.940
so whatever permissions that are granted to&nbsp;
that role apply to everyone on a server. Thus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:21.840
one needs to be very careful about any&nbsp;
permissions assigned to the public role.

